QT.sseContent = [
  {"id":"q-prob-001","track":"probability","tags":["expectation","dice","distribution-of-max"],"difficulty":2,"time":120,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Two fair dice are rolled. What is the expected value of the maximum?","hints":["Find P(max ≤ k) first.","P(max = k) = P(max ≤ k) − P(max ≤ k−1)."],"answer":"161/36","percent":false,"solution":"<p>P(max ≤ k) = (k/6)² = k²/36, so P(max = k) = (k² − (k−1)²)/36 = (2k−1)/36.</p><p>E = Σₖ k(2k−1)/36 = (2Σk² − Σk)/36 = (2·91 − 21)/36 = 161/36 ≈ 4.47.</p>","value":4.4722,"tol":0.01},
  {"id":"q-prob-002","track":"probability","tags":["conditional","classic"],"difficulty":1,"time":60,"type":"numeric","prompt":"A family has two children and at least one is a boy. What is the probability both are boys?","hints":["List the equally likely outcomes.","The condition rules out only GG."],"answer":"1/3","percent":false,"solution":"<p>Equally likely outcomes: BB, BG, GB, GG. The condition removes GG, leaving 3, of which 1 (BB) has two boys. P = 1/3.</p>","value":0.3333,"tol":0.005},
  {"id":"q-prob-003","track":"probability","tags":["conditional","classic","monty-hall"],"difficulty":1,"time":60,"type":"numeric","prompt":"In the Monty Hall problem, what is the probability of winning if you switch?","hints":["How often is your first pick right?","The host's reveal never changes that."],"answer":"2/3","percent":false,"solution":"<p>Your first pick is right with probability 1/3. The host always opens a goat door, which gives no new information about your door. Switching wins exactly when your first pick was wrong: 2/3.</p>","value":0.6667,"tol":0.005},
  {"id":"q-prob-004","track":"probability","tags":["bayes","base-rates"],"difficulty":2,"time":120,"type":"numeric","prompt":"A disease affects 1% of people. A test detects 99% of true cases and gives false positives for 5% of healthy people. Given a positive test, what is P(disease)?","hints":["Use Bayes. Compute P(+) using the law of total probability.","P(+) = 0.01·0.99 + 0.99·0.05."],"answer":"1/6","percent":false,"solution":"<p>P(+) = 0.0099 + 0.0495 = 0.0594. P(D|+) = 0.0099/0.0594 = 1/6 ≈ 16.7%.</p><p>False positives from the large healthy group swamp the true positives.</p>","value":0.1667,"tol":0.005},
  {"id":"q-prob-005","track":"probability","tags":["coupon-collector","expectation","geometric"],"difficulty":3,"time":150,"type":"numeric","prompt":"What is the expected number of rolls of a fair die to see every face at least once?","hints":["Split the process into stages: waiting for the 1st new face, the 2nd, and so on.","With k faces already seen, the wait for a new one is Geometric((6−k)/6)."],"answer":"14.7","percent":false,"solution":"<p>Stage k (having seen k faces) has expected length 6/(6−k). Sum = 6(1 + 1/2 + 1/3 + 1/4 + 1/5 + 1/6) = 6 × 2.45 = 14.7.</p>","value":14.7,"tol":0.05},
  {"id":"q-prob-006","track":"probability","tags":["first-step-analysis","coins"],"difficulty":3,"time":150,"type":"numeric","prompt":"What is the expected number of fair coin flips until you get two heads in a row?","hints":["Define E (from scratch) and E₁ (just flipped an H).","E = 1 + ½E₁ + ½E and E₁ = 1 + ½·0 + ½E."],"answer":"6","percent":false,"solution":"<p>From E₁ = 1 + ½E, substitute into E = 1 + ½(1 + ½E) + ½E = 1.5 + ¾E, so ¼E = 1.5 and E = 6.</p><p>(For HT the answer is 4, because a failed attempt still leaves you with an H.)</p>","value":6,"tol":0},
  {"id":"q-prob-007","track":"probability","tags":["first-step-analysis","dice"],"difficulty":3,"time":150,"type":"numeric","prompt":"What is the expected number of die rolls until two 6s in a row?","hints":["You need a 6 first, which takes 6 rolls on average.","After that 6, one more roll either finishes or sends you back to the start."],"answer":"42","percent":false,"solution":"<p>E = 6 + 1 + (5/6)E, so (1/6)E = 7 and E = 42.</p>","value":42,"tol":0},
  {"id":"q-prob-008","track":"probability","tags":["symmetry","cards","expectation"],"difficulty":3,"time":120,"type":"numeric","prompt":"A standard deck is shuffled. What is the expected position of the first ace?","hints":["The 4 aces split the other 48 cards into 5 gaps.","By symmetry, every gap has the same expected size."],"answer":"10.6","percent":false,"solution":"<p>Each of the 5 gaps holds 48/5 = 9.6 cards on average. The first ace comes right after the first gap: 9.6 + 1 = 10.6 = (52+1)/(4+1).</p>","value":10.6,"tol":0.01},
  {"id":"q-prob-009","track":"probability","tags":["geometric-probability","continuous"],"difficulty":4,"time":240,"type":"numeric","prompt":"A stick is broken at two uniformly random points. What is the probability the three pieces form a triangle?","hints":["The triangle inequality means every piece must be shorter than ½.","Draw the unit square of (x, y) and shade the region where that holds."],"answer":"1/4","percent":false,"solution":"<p>Take x &lt; y (half the square, by symmetry). The pieces are x, y−x and 1−y, and each must be &lt; ½, so x &lt; ½, y &gt; ½ and y − x &lt; ½. This region is a triangle of area 1/8 inside the half-square of area ½, giving 1/4.</p>","value":0.25,"tol":0.005},
  {"id":"q-prob-010","track":"probability","tags":["symmetry","classic"],"difficulty":1,"time":60,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Three ants sit at the corners of a triangle and each picks a random direction along an edge. What is the probability that at least two collide?","hints":["When is there no collision?","Only when all three move the same way."],"answer":"3/4","percent":false,"solution":"<p>There are 2³ = 8 equally likely direction choices. Only 2 (all clockwise or all anticlockwise) avoid a collision. P = 1 − 2/8 = 3/4.</p>","value":0.75,"tol":0.005},
  {"id":"q-prob-011","track":"probability","tags":["random-walk","gamblers-ruin","martingale"],"difficulty":3,"time":120,"type":"numeric","prompt":"A fair ±1 random walk starts at 1 and stops at 0 or 3. What is the probability it reaches 3?","hints":["For a fair walk, the expected position never changes (it's a martingale).","1 = 3p + 0(1−p)."],"answer":"1/3","percent":false,"solution":"<p>The expected final position equals the start: 3p = 1, so p = 1/3. In general, P(hit N before 0 | start at k) = k/N.</p>","value":0.3333,"tol":0.005},
  {"id":"q-prob-012","track":"probability","tags":["order-statistics","uniform","continuous"],"difficulty":2,"time":90,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Two independent Uniform(0,1) values are drawn. What is the expected value of the larger one?","hints":["P(max ≤ x) = x².","Differentiate to get the density, then integrate x·f(x)."],"answer":"2/3","percent":false,"solution":"<p>The density is 2x, so E = ∫₀¹ x·2x dx = 2/3. In general, E[max of n] = n/(n+1), and the minimum of two has expectation 1/3.</p>","value":0.6667,"tol":0.005},
  {"id":"q-prob-013","track":"probability","tags":["linearity","indicators","permutations"],"difficulty":2,"time":90,"type":"numeric","prompt":"A deck of n cards is shuffled uniformly at random. What is the expected number of cards that end up in their original position?","hints":["Let Iᵢ = 1 if card i stays in place.","Use linearity. Independence isn't needed."],"answer":"1","percent":false,"solution":"<p>E[Iᵢ] = 1/n. Summing over n cards gives 1, for any n.</p>","value":1,"tol":0},
  {"id":"q-ev-001","track":"expected-value","tags":["optimal-stopping","dice"],"difficulty":2,"time":90,"type":"numeric","prompt":"You roll a die and may reroll once, keeping the final value. What is the fair price of this game?","hints":["A reroll is worth 3.5.","Keep any roll above 3.5."],"answer":"4.25","percent":false,"solution":"<p>Keep 4, 5, 6 (average 5, with probability ½). Otherwise reroll for 3.5. EV = ½·5 + ½·3.5 = 4.25.</p>","value":4.25,"tol":0.01},
  {"id":"q-ev-002","track":"expected-value","tags":["optimal-stopping","dice","backward-induction"],"difficulty":3,"time":120,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Same game, but you may reroll up to twice. What is the fair price?","hints":["Work backwards. With one reroll left, the game is worth 4.25.","On the first roll, keep only results above 4.25."],"answer":"14/3","percent":false,"solution":"<p>Keep 5 or 6 (average 5.5, with probability 1/3). Otherwise continue for 4.25. EV = (1/3)·5.5 + (2/3)·4.25 = 1.8333 + 2.8333 = 14/3.</p>","value":4.6667,"tol":0.01},
  {"id":"q-ev-003","track":"expected-value","tags":["pricing","dice","second-moment"],"difficulty":1,"time":60,"type":"numeric","prompt":"A game pays the square of one die roll. What is the fair price?","hints":["You need E[X²], not E[X]².","1+4+9+16+25+36 = 91."],"answer":"91/6","percent":false,"solution":"<p>E[X²] = 91/6 ≈ 15.17. Note that E[X]² = 12.25 is lower. The gap is the variance, 35/12.</p>","value":15.1667,"tol":0.01},
  {"id":"q-ev-004","track":"expected-value","tags":["kelly","sizing","risk"],"difficulty":2,"time":90,"type":"numeric","prompt":"You win 60% of even-money bets. What fraction of your bankroll should you bet each time to maximise long-run growth?","hints":["Kelly: f* = (bp − q)/b.","Here b = 1."],"answer":"0.2","percent":false,"solution":"<p>f* = 0.6 − 0.4 = 0.2, so bet 20%. In practice people often use half Kelly (10%) to cut variance while keeping about 75% of the growth rate.</p>","value":0.2,"tol":0.005},
  {"id":"q-mm-001","track":"market-making","tags":["quoting","updating","dice"],"difficulty":2,"time":120,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Make a market on the sum of two dice. The counterparty buys at your offer, then tells you one die shows a 1. Re-quote.","hints":["The initial fair value is 7.","After the reveal, fair = 1 + 3.5."],"answer":"6.5 at 7.5, then 4 at 5","percent":false,"solution":"<p>Fair is 7 and the SD is √(2·35/12) ≈ 2.4, so quote 6.5 at 7.5. You're now short one at 7.5. After the reveal, fair = 4.5, so your short is +3 in expectation. Re-quote around 4.5: 4 at 5. Buying back at 5 locks in a profit of 2.5.</p>","value":4.5,"tol":0.5},
  {"id":"q-mm-002","track":"market-making","tags":["quoting","binomial"],"difficulty":2,"time":90,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Make a market on the number of heads in 10 fair coin flips.","hints":["Binomial(10, ½).","SD = √(np(1−p))."],"answer":"4.5 at 5.5","percent":false,"solution":"<p>Fair is 5 and the SD is √2.5 ≈ 1.58. A width of about 1 is reasonable, so quote 4.5 at 5.5. Widen if you're unsure the coin is fair.</p>","value":5,"tol":0.5},
  {"id":"q-mm-003","track":"market-making","tags":["adverse-selection","inventory"],"difficulty":3,"time":90,"type":"conceptual","prompt":"Your offer is lifted five times in a row. What do you do and why?","hints":["What does one-directional flow suggest?","Think about fair value, width and position separately."],"answer":"Raise fair value, widen, skew up","percent":false,"solution":"<p>Persistent buying suggests informed flow (adverse selection), so revise fair value upwards. Widen while you're uncertain. You're also now short five, so skew your quotes up to attract sellers and discourage more buyers. Never just re-quote the same market.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-mm-arith-001","track":"mental-maths","tags":["multiplication"],"difficulty":1,"time":10,"type":"numeric","prompt":"64 × 15","hints":["64 × 10 + 64 × 5","640 + 320"],"answer":"960","percent":false,"solution":"<p>640 + 320 = 960.</p>","value":960,"tol":0},
  {"id":"q-mm-arith-002","track":"mental-maths","tags":["percentages"],"difficulty":1,"time":10,"type":"numeric","prompt":"7.5% of 480","hints":["10% − 2.5%","48 − 12"],"answer":"36","percent":false,"solution":"<p>10% of 480 = 48 and 2.5% = 12, so 48 − 12 = 36.</p>","value":36,"tol":0},
  {"id":"q-mm-arith-003","track":"mental-maths","tags":["squares"],"difficulty":1,"time":10,"type":"numeric","prompt":"99²","hints":["(100 − 1)²","10000 − 200 + 1"],"answer":"9801","percent":false,"solution":"<p>10000 − 200 + 1 = 9801.</p>","value":9801,"tol":0},
  {"id":"q-mm-arith-004","track":"mental-maths","tags":["percent-change","fractions"],"difficulty":2,"time":15,"type":"numeric","prompt":"A stock moves from 84 to 91. What is the percentage change?","hints":["Change / start.","7/84 = 1/12."],"answer":"8.33%","percent":true,"solution":"<p>7/84 = 1/12 ≈ 8.33%.</p>","value":8.333,"tol":0.01},
  {"id":"q-mm-arith-005","track":"mental-maths","tags":["multiplication","powers-of-2"],"difficulty":1,"time":10,"type":"numeric","prompt":"125 × 32","hints":["125 × 8 = 1000","32 = 8 × 4"],"answer":"4000","percent":false,"solution":"<p>1000 × 4 = 4000.</p>","value":4000,"tol":0},
  {"id":"q-fermi-001","track":"fermi","tags":["consumer","decomposition"],"difficulty":2,"time":180,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Estimate the number of cups of coffee drunk in the UK per day.","hints":["Population × share of coffee drinkers × cups per drinker.","Cross-check using the number of cafés."],"answer":"About 100 million","percent":false,"solution":"<p>About 50m adults × 60% drinkers × 2 cups ≈ 60m a day. Cross-check: at 300 cups per café per day, 60m cups would need 200k outlets, which is too many for cafés alone, so most coffee is made at home. That's consistent. The answer is most sensitive to the share of people who drink coffee.</p><p>Reference: the British Coffee Association puts UK consumption at about 103 million cups a day, so this estimate is within a factor of 2.</p>","value":103000000,"logTol":0.3},
  {"id":"q-beh-001","track":"behavioural","tags":["why-trading"],"difficulty":2,"time":120,"type":"conceptual","prompt":"Why trading?","hints":[],"answer":"Self-graded","percent":false,"solution":"<p>Model structure: what draws you (decision-making under uncertainty, fast feedback) → evidence (projects, competitions, games of incomplete information such as poker) → why now.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-beh-002","track":"behavioural","tags":["why-firm","citadel"],"difficulty":2,"time":120,"type":"conceptual","prompt":"Why Citadel rather than Citadel Securities or a bank?","hints":[],"answer":"Self-graded","percent":false,"solution":"<p>Model structure: a correct statement of fund vs market maker → specific reasons (multi-strategy, investment in technology and data, risk discipline) → fit with your own skills.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-beh-003","track":"behavioural","tags":["uncertainty","star"],"difficulty":2,"time":120,"type":"conceptual","prompt":"Tell me about a decision you made with incomplete information.","hints":[],"answer":"Self-graded","percent":false,"solution":"<p>STAR. The Result must be measurable, and close with what you'd do differently.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-beh-004","track":"behavioural","tags":["project-deep-dive"],"difficulty":2,"time":120,"type":"conceptual","prompt":"Walk me through your most technical project. What was the hardest design decision?","hints":[],"answer":"Self-graded","percent":false,"solution":"<p>Model structure: problem → approach → one key decision and its trade-off → result → a mistake you fixed. Expect follow-up questions on every claim.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-stat-001","track":"statistics-regression","tags":["regression","beta","correlation"],"difficulty":1,"time":60,"type":"numeric","prompt":"corr(X,Y) = 0.6, σ_Y = 20%, σ_X = 10%. What is the OLS slope when regressing Y on X?","hints":["β = Cov(X,Y)/Var(X).","Rewrite this as ρ·σ_Y/σ_X."],"answer":"1.2","percent":false,"solution":"<p>β = ρ·σ_Y/σ_X = 0.6 × 20/10 = 1.2.</p>","value":1.2,"tol":0.001},
  {"id":"q-stat-002","track":"statistics-regression","tags":["regression","correlation","symmetry"],"difficulty":2,"time":90,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Regressing Y on X gives slope 0.8. Regressing X on Y gives slope 0.45. What is corr(X,Y)?","hints":["Write both slopes in terms of ρ, σ_X and σ_Y.","Multiply the two slopes together."],"answer":"0.6","percent":false,"solution":"<p>β_YX·β_XY = (ρσ_Y/σ_X)(ρσ_X/σ_Y) = ρ². So ρ² = 0.36 and ρ = 0.6. It is positive because both slopes are positive.</p><p>Follow-up insight: the two regression lines are different lines unless |ρ| = 1.</p>","value":0.6,"tol":0.001},
  {"id":"q-stat-003","track":"statistics-regression","tags":["correlation-matrix","psd","bounds"],"difficulty":3,"time":150,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Three random variables all have the same pairwise correlation ρ. What is the smallest possible value of ρ?","hints":["Var(X+Y+Z) ≥ 0.","Use standardised variables. The variance of the sum is 3 + 6ρ."],"answer":"-1/2","percent":false,"solution":"<p>For standardised X, Y, Z: Var(X+Y+Z) = 3 + 6ρ ≥ 0, so ρ ≥ −1/2. In general, n equicorrelated variables need ρ ≥ −1/(n−1). (Equivalently, the correlation matrix must be positive semi-definite.)</p>","value":-0.5,"tol":0.001},
  {"id":"q-stat-004","track":"statistics-regression","tags":["correlation-matrix","psd","bounds"],"difficulty":4,"time":180,"type":"numeric","prompt":"corr(X,Y) = 0.9 and corr(Y,Z) = 0.9. What is the minimum possible corr(X,Z)?","hints":["The 3×3 correlation matrix must have a non-negative determinant.","Bound: ρ_XZ ≥ ρ_XY·ρ_YZ − √((1−ρ_XY²)(1−ρ_YZ²))."],"answer":"0.62","percent":false,"solution":"<p>ρ_XZ ≥ 0.81 − √(0.19 × 0.19) = 0.81 − 0.19 = 0.62.</p><p>Intuition: in angle terms, each pair is cos⁻¹(0.9) ≈ 25.8° apart, so X and Z are at most 51.7° apart, and cos 51.7° ≈ 0.62.</p>","value":0.62,"tol":0.005},
  {"id":"q-stat-005","track":"statistics-regression","tags":["correlation","normal"],"difficulty":2,"time":90,"type":"numeric","prompt":"X and Y are independent N(0,1). What is corr(X, X+Y)?","hints":["Cov(X, X+Y) = Var(X) + Cov(X,Y).","Var(X+Y) = 2."],"answer":"1/√2","percent":false,"solution":"<p>Cov = 1 and the SDs are 1 and √2, so ρ = 1/√2 ≈ 0.707.</p>","value":0.7071,"tol":0.001},
  {"id":"q-stat-006","track":"statistics-regression","tags":["sharpe","annualisation"],"difficulty":1,"time":60,"type":"numeric","prompt":"A strategy averages 0.05% a day with a daily SD of 1%, and daily returns are independent. What is its annualised Sharpe ratio (252 trading days)? Ignore the risk-free rate.","hints":["The mean scales with T and the SD with √T.","Sharpe_annual = Sharpe_daily × √252."],"answer":"≈ 0.79","percent":false,"solution":"<p>Daily Sharpe = 0.05/1 = 0.05, and × √252 ≈ 15.87 gives about 0.79.</p><p>This assumes independent returns. Autocorrelation breaks the √T scaling.</p>","value":0.794,"tol":0.01},
  {"id":"q-stat-007","track":"statistics-regression","tags":["multiple-testing","false-discovery"],"difficulty":2,"time":90,"type":"numeric","prompt":"You test 100 signals that are pure noise, each at the 5% significance level, independently. What is the probability that at least one looks significant?","hints":["Use the complement.","0.95¹⁰⁰ ≈ e⁻⁵."],"answer":"≈ 99.4%","percent":true,"solution":"<p>1 − 0.95¹⁰⁰ ≈ 1 − 0.0059 = 0.994. You'd expect about 5 false positives.</p><p>Remedies: Bonferroni correction (α/n), a held-out test set, and a requirement for an economic rationale.</p>","value":0.994,"tol":0.002},
  {"id":"q-stat-008","track":"statistics-regression","tags":["r-squared","regression"],"difficulty":1,"time":45,"type":"numeric","prompt":"In a simple linear regression, corr(X,Y) = 0.3. What is R²?","hints":["For one regressor, R² = ρ²."],"answer":"0.09","percent":false,"solution":"<p>R² = 0.3² = 0.09, so X explains 9% of the variance in Y. In finance this is often a meaningful signal, since daily return predictability is tiny.</p>","value":0.09,"tol":0.001},
  {"id":"q-stat-009","track":"statistics-regression","tags":["portfolio","diversification","variance"],"difficulty":2,"time":90,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Two assets each have 20% volatility and a correlation of 0.5. What is the volatility of a 50/50 portfolio?","hints":["Var = w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2w₁w₂ρσ₁σ₂.","Each term works out to 0.01."],"answer":"≈ 17.3%","percent":true,"solution":"<p>Var = 0.25(0.04) + 0.25(0.04) + 2(0.25)(0.5)(0.04) = 0.03, and √0.03 ≈ 17.3%.</p>","value":0.1732,"tol":0.002},
  {"id":"q-stat-010","track":"statistics-regression","tags":["standard-error","clt"],"difficulty":2,"time":90,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Daily returns have an SD of 1%. Over one year (252 days), what is the standard error of the estimated mean daily return, in %?","hints":["SE = σ/√n.","√252 ≈ 15.87."],"answer":"≈ 0.063%","percent":true,"solution":"<p>1%/15.87 ≈ 0.063%. That's larger than a typical edge of around 0.05% a day, which is why a year of data rarely proves anything.</p>","value":0.063,"tol":0.001},
  {"id":"q-stat-011","track":"statistics-regression","tags":["multicollinearity","regression"],"difficulty":2,"time":120,"type":"conceptual","prompt":"You add a feature that is an exact copy of an existing feature to an OLS regression. What happens to the coefficients, the fit, and the predictions?","hints":["Think about whether XᵀX can be inverted.","Can the coefficients be uniquely determined?"],"answer":"Coefficients become unidentified; fit and predictions are unchanged","percent":false,"solution":"<p>XᵀX becomes singular, so OLS has no unique solution. Any split β₁ + β₂ = β_original fits equally well. R² and predictions are unchanged. With near-duplicates, the coefficients have huge variance and swing in sign. Fixes: drop one feature, combine them, or use ridge regularisation.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-stat-012","track":"statistics-regression","tags":["pca","factors","equities"],"difficulty":2,"time":120,"type":"conceptual","prompt":"You run PCA on the daily returns of 500 stocks. What does the first principal component usually represent, and why?","hints":["PCA finds the direction of maximum variance.","What drives most stocks at the same time?"],"answer":"The market factor","percent":false,"solution":"<p>PC1 is roughly an equal-sign weighting of all stocks, which is the market, because the common market move is the largest shared source of variance. Later components often match sectors or styles. Stat-arb uses this by trading the residuals after removing the top components.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-eq-001","track":"equity-quant","tags":["backtesting","overfitting","scepticism"],"difficulty":3,"time":180,"type":"conceptual","prompt":"A colleague shows you a backtest of a daily equity strategy with an annualised Sharpe ratio of 3. What do you check before believing it?","hints":["Think about data problems, then costs, then statistics.","Look-ahead, survivorship, costs, overfitting, capacity, crowding."],"answer":"Look-ahead, survivorship, costs, overfitting, capacity, crowding, regime","percent":false,"solution":"<ol><li><b>Look-ahead bias:</b> was any data used before it was actually available (closing prices used at the close, restated fundamentals)?</li><li><b>Survivorship bias:</b> does the stock universe include companies that were later delisted?</li><li><b>Costs:</b> spread, market impact and borrow costs for shorts. Many high-Sharpe strategies are really high-turnover strategies.</li><li><b>Overfitting:</b> how many variants were tried? Is there out-of-sample or walk-forward evidence?</li><li><b>Capacity:</b> does it still work at realistic size?</li><li><b>Crowding/regime:</b> is it a known factor exposure that can unwind sharply?</li><li><b>Economic rationale:</b> why should the edge exist, and who is on the other side?</li></ol>"},
  {"id":"q-eq-002","track":"equity-quant","tags":["signals","momentum","mean-reversion"],"difficulty":1,"time":90,"type":"conceptual","prompt":"Explain the difference between momentum and short-term reversal in equities, and the typical horizon of each.","hints":["One says trends persist, the other says moves overshoot.","Classic momentum uses about 12 months of returns and skips the most recent month."],"answer":"Momentum ≈ 3–12 months persists; reversal ≈ days–1 month reverts","percent":false,"solution":"<p><b>Momentum:</b> stocks with strong 3–12 month returns tend to keep outperforming (classically 12-month returns skipping the last month). Explanations: underreaction and slow diffusion of information.</p><p><b>Short-term reversal:</b> last week's or month's winners tend to underperform briefly. Explanations: liquidity provision and overreaction.</p><p>The skipped month in the momentum signal exists to avoid the reversal effect.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-eq-003","track":"equity-quant","tags":["hedging","beta","neutrality"],"difficulty":1,"time":60,"type":"numeric","prompt":"You are long $10m of a stock with β = 1.5 to the index. How much index exposure must you short to be beta-neutral, in $m?","hints":["Beta exposure = position × β."],"answer":"$15m","percent":false,"solution":"<p>$10m × 1.5 = $15m of index exposure, so short $15m of index futures. What's left is the stock-specific (idiosyncratic) return, plus any sector or style exposure you haven't hedged.</p>","value":15,"tol":0.01},
  {"id":"q-eq-004","track":"equity-quant","tags":["market-impact","square-root-law","costs"],"difficulty":2,"time":90,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Under the square-root impact model, trading 1% of a stock's daily volume costs 10 bps of impact. How much impact, in bps, does trading 4% of daily volume cost?","hints":["Impact ∝ σ·√(Q/V).","Four times the size gives √4 times the impact."],"answer":"20 bps","percent":false,"solution":"<p>10 × √4 = 20 bps. Total cost (impact × size) therefore grows like Q^1.5, so doubling a strategy's size more than doubles its costs. This is the root of capacity limits.</p>","value":20,"tol":0.01},
  {"id":"q-eq-005","track":"equity-quant","tags":["turnover","costs","net-alpha"],"difficulty":2,"time":90,"type":"numeric","prompt":"A strategy has 20% annual gross alpha. It trades 50× its book value a year at an all-in cost of 10 bps per unit traded. What is its net annual alpha, in %?","hints":["Annual cost = turnover × cost per unit.","50 × 0.10% = ?"],"answer":"15%","percent":true,"solution":"<p>Cost = 50 × 0.10% = 5% a year, so net = 20% − 5% = 15%. If costs doubled to 20 bps, net alpha would fall from 15% to 10%. Execution quality is therefore a direct source of alpha, which is a core reason this desk exists.</p>","value":15,"tol":0.01},
  {"id":"q-eq-006","track":"equity-quant","tags":["neutrality","risk","factors"],"difficulty":2,"time":120,"type":"conceptual","prompt":"Why would a systematic equity strategy be built to be beta-neutral and sector-neutral?","hints":["What return are you actually trying to capture?","What happens to an unhedged long-tech, short-energy book in a sector rotation?"],"answer":"To isolate the signal from unrewarded common-factor risk","percent":false,"solution":"<p>The goal is to earn the <b>signal's</b> return, not market or sector moves. Unhedged exposure adds large variance with no expected return from your model, which lowers the Sharpe ratio. It also creates concentrated risks (a sector rotation can wipe out months of alpha). Neutralising also makes performance attribution clean.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-eq-007","track":"equity-quant","tags":["crowding","semi-systematic","judgement"],"difficulty":3,"time":150,"type":"conceptual","prompt":"What can a semi-systematic trader add that a fully systematic strategy can't? Give a concrete example.","hints":["What information does the model not have?","Crowding, events, regime changes, execution."],"answer":"Judgement on regime, crowding, events and execution","percent":false,"solution":"<p>Models learn from historical data, so they're weakest when conditions change. A trader can:</p><ul><li><b>reduce exposure</b> to a crowded factor before an unwind (e.g. a sharp reversal in momentum and quant long-short positioning)</li><li><b>avoid events</b> the model doesn't understand (M&amp;A, earnings, index rebalances, regulatory news)</li><li><b>improve execution</b> by timing trades and choosing venues and algorithms</li><li><b>decide sizing</b> using real-time context on risk.</li></ul><p>The trade-off is that discretion can bring back bias, so the trader's overrides must be measured too.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-py-001","track":"python-drills","tags":["pandas","returns","volatility"],"difficulty":1,"time":300,"type":"code-task","prompt":"Write <code>ann_vol(prices: pd.Series) -&gt; float</code> that returns the annualised volatility of daily simple returns (252 days, sample SD).","hints":["<code>prices.pct_change().dropna()</code>","Multiply the SD by <code>np.sqrt(252)</code>."],"answer":"<code>prices.pct_change().dropna().std() * np.sqrt(252)</code>","percent":false,"solution":"<pre class=\"code-view\" tabindex=\"0\">import numpy as np, pandas as pd\ndef ann_vol(prices: pd.Series) -&gt; float:\n    r = prices.pct_change().dropna()\n    return r.std(ddof=1) * np.sqrt(252)</pre>","tests":"prices = [100, 101, 99, 102, 103] → compare with the reference implementation, tolerance 1e-9"},
  {"id":"q-py-002","track":"python-drills","tags":["backtest","lookahead","pandas"],"difficulty":2,"time":600,"type":"code-task","prompt":"Given daily <code>prices</code>, go long 1 unit when the 5-day moving average is above the 20-day moving average, and stay flat otherwise. Return the series of daily strategy returns <b>without look-ahead bias</b>.","hints":["A signal computed from today's close can only earn tomorrow's return.","<code>.shift(1)</code> the position."],"answer":"position = (ma5 &gt; ma20).astype(int).shift(1); strat = position * returns","percent":false,"solution":"<pre class=\"code-view\" tabindex=\"0\">def ma_cross_returns(prices: pd.Series) -&gt; pd.Series:\n    r = prices.pct_change()\n    signal = (prices.rolling(5).mean() &gt; prices.rolling(20).mean()).astype(int)\n    position = signal.shift(1)          # trade on the next bar, so no look-ahead\n    return (position * r).fillna(0.0)</pre><p>Common bug: forgetting <code>.shift(1)</code>, which makes the strategy use information from the same day it trades.</p>","tests":"synthetic random-walk prices (seed 0, 300 days) → compare with the reference; also assert that the first 20 values are 0 or NaN"},
  {"id":"q-py-003","track":"python-drills","tags":["numpy","correlation","rolling"],"difficulty":2,"time":300,"type":"code-task","prompt":"Given two price series <code>a</code> and <code>b</code>, return their 60-day rolling correlation of daily returns.","hints":["Convert to returns first. Correlating prices gives misleading results.","<code>ra.rolling(60).corr(rb)</code>"],"answer":"<code>a.pct_change().rolling(60).corr(b.pct_change())</code>","percent":false,"solution":"<pre class=\"code-view\" tabindex=\"0\">def rolling_corr(a: pd.Series, b: pd.Series, w: int = 60) -&gt; pd.Series:\n    return a.pct_change().rolling(w).corr(b.pct_change())</pre><p>Interview point: two prices that both trend upwards look highly correlated even when their returns are unrelated (spurious correlation from non-stationary series).</p>","tests":"two correlated synthetic series (seed 1) → compare with the reference, tolerance 1e-9"},
  {"id":"q-proj-001","track":"behavioural","tags":["project-deep-dive","poker","bayesian"],"difficulty":3,"time":180,"type":"conceptual","prompt":"Walk me through your poker opponent-modelling project. How does the model update its beliefs, and how do you decide how much to exploit those beliefs?","hints":["Prior → observation → posterior → action.","Exploitation vs exploitability, with the equilibrium as the baseline."],"answer":"Self-graded against the structure below","percent":false,"solution":"<p>Structure: the problem (cold-start modelling in heads-up restricted-action NLHE) → the prior and its justification → the update rule per observed action → how confidence drives the deviation from equilibrium → the tournament comparison (round-robin vs Swiss) → one result → one mistake fixed.</p><p>Trading link: the posterior's confidence sets the bet size, like the strength of a signal setting position size. Over-exploiting a weak read is like over-sizing a noisy signal.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-proj-002","track":"behavioural","tags":["why-role","semi-systematic"],"difficulty":2,"time":120,"type":"conceptual","prompt":"You applied to Investment &amp; Trading. Why does a semi-systematic equities role suit you?","hints":["Link your CS and quantitative skills to the systematic side, and your decision-making to the discretionary side."],"answer":"Self-graded","percent":false,"solution":"<p>Structure: the systematic side matches the CS, statistics and modelling skills you've shown → the discretionary side matches decision-making under uncertainty (poker, real-time leadership) → genuine interest in where models fail and judgement adds value (see q-eq-007).</p>"},
];
